**user**:
> What is it called when government pays money and crowds-in private finance, or the ratio that you get from it?
**assistant**:
> You’re looking for two related ideas:
> 
> 1. **The mechanism:**  
>    When government spending “pulls in” additional private investment, it’s usually called:
> 
>    - **Crowding in private investment / private finance**  
>    - More technically in climate/infra work: **“mobilizing private capital”** or **“leverage of public finance.”**
> 
>    Phrasings you’ll see in reports:
>    - “This program mobilized $X of private capital for every $1 of public funding.”
>    - “The intervention significantly crowded in private investment.”
> 
> 2. **The ratio:**  
>    The standard name for the ratio is:
> 
>    - **Leverage ratio** (or **public-to-private leverage ratio**)  
>    - Sometimes **“mobilization ratio”** or **“crowding-in ratio”** in more narrative text.
> 
>    Formally, people often define it as:
>    \[
>    \text{Leverage ratio} = \frac{\text{Private capital mobilized}}{\text{Public capital deployed}}
>    \]
>    So if $1 of concessional/public money brings in $5 of private money, that’s a **5:1 leverage ratio** (or “$5 of private for each $1 of public”).
> 
